[# Section 5 — the CX code language.
   Anchored in spec/code.md (post-rename), spec/cxpath_alignment.md, ADRs
   0027/0028/0029/0030. Largest section in the guide. #]

[section id=code n=5 title='The code language'

  [child id=overview n=5.1 title='The CX code language'
    [intro
      """CX code is CX data with a leading `?` on the element name. A
         directive like `[?for]` is a normal element to the parser; the
         evaluator recognizes the `?` prefix and dispatches on the
         directive name. Everything else — patterns, paths, function
         calls, builtins — is built from ordinary CX shapes."""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[# Data is data:                       #]
         [user :id 1 :email 'ada@example.com']

         [# Code is data, plus a `?`:           #]
         [?for $u :in //user :yield $u@email]"""]

    [body
      """The evaluator is `cx_code_eval(doc, code, target_format)` at the
         C ABI level; every binding's Layer-1 `Doc.eval(code)` wraps it
         (see [[bindings]]). The full normative spec is
         [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md). This section is the
         working developer guide."""]

    [body
      """Code can do five things: **select** (CXPath, see [[cxpath]]),
         **iterate** (`[?for]`, see [[for]]), **dispatch**
         (`[?match]`, see [[match]]), **bind** (`[?let]` / `[?def]` /
         `[?fn]`), and **update** (`[?modify]`, see [[modify]]). Every
         other directive is one of these five lifted into a specific
         domain — concurrency, async, resilience, services."""]]

  [child id=directives n=5.2 title='Directive registry'
    [intro
      """v0.8.0 has **39 normative directives**. Adding or removing any
         directive requires an ADR (per
         [`spec/governance.md`](../../spec/governance.md) §10). The full
         registry is in [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md) §4.1."""]

    [body
      """Core constructs (11):"""]

    [list
      [item """`[?match]` — pattern dispatch, single- and multi-arm
              (see [[match]])"""]
      [item """`[?modify]` — pure-functional update (see [[modify]])"""]
      [item """`[?for]` — iteration / comprehension (see [[for]])"""]
      [item """`[?let]` — local binding (see [[let]])"""]
      [item """`[?fn]` — anonymous function"""]
      [item """`[?def]` — named definition"""]
      [item """`[?if]` — conditional with EBV (see [[if]])"""]
      [item """`[?try]` — error handling block"""]
      [item """`[?pipe]` — pipeline (canonical form of `|`)"""]
      [item """`[?par-map]` — parallel map"""]
      [item """`[?par-reduce]` — parallel reduce"""]]

    [body
      """Resilience directives (6): `[?retry]`, `[?timeout]`,
         `[?circuit-breaker]`, `[?fallback]`, `[?rate-limit]`,
         `[?bulkhead]` (see [[resilience]])."""]

    [body
      """Services + concurrency + async (22): `[?service]`,
         `[?service-handle]`, `[?http-client]`, `[?worker]`,
         `[?worker-handle]`, `[?channel]`, `[?send]`, `[?receive]`,
         `[?try-send]`, `[?try-receive]`, `[?close]`, `[?select]`,
         `[?stop]`, `[?wait-for]`, `[?async]`, `[?await]`,
         `[?await-all]`, `[?await-any]`, `[?await-race]`, `[?cancel]`,
         `[?check-cancel]`, `[?sleep]` (see [[services]], [[workers]],
         [[async]])."""]

    [note
      """**Retired in v0.8.0.** `[?find]` is no longer a directive. Use
         `[?for]` with a pattern, or CXPath `//pattern`. See
         [[migration]] for mechanical migration rules and ADR
         [0028](../../spec/decisions/0028-cxpath-as-value-kind.md) D8
         for the rationale."""]]

  [child id=cxpath n=5.3 title='CXPath — the universal selector'
    [intro
      """CXPath is the single selector vocabulary across reading,
         iteration, dispatch, and update. It aligns with XPath 3.1
         (`spec/cxpath_alignment.md`) with deliberate divergences kept
         minimal. **Path** is a value kind, not parser sugar — paths
         compile, cache, round-trip, and visualize as themselves."""]

    [body
      """The simplest CXPath is the descendant search:"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """//user                       [# every user, anywhere in doc      #]
         //user[@active=true]         [# every active user                #]
         //user/@email                [# scalar attribute values          #]
         //section[1]                 [# first section                    #]
         //section[h2]                [# section that has an h2 child     #]"""]

    [body
      """The grammar is the XPath 3.1 **selection core**:"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """PathExpr   ::= '//' StepList | '/' StepList | StepList
         Step       ::= ( Axis '::' )? NodeTest Predicate*
         Predicate  ::= '[' PredExpr ']'
         PredExpr   ::= AttrTest | INT | FunctionCall | BooleanExpr"""]

    [body
      """Twelve axes are supported: `child`, `descendant`,
         `descendant-or-self`, `parent`, `ancestor`,
         `ancestor-or-self`, `following-sibling`, `preceding-sibling`,
         `following`, `preceding`, `self`, `attribute`. The default
         axis is `child` (so `name` ≡ `child::name`)."""]

    [body
      """**Comparison.** One operator syntax: `= != < <= > >=`. No
         keyword synonyms (`eq`/`ne`/...) — they are not in the
         grammar. Semantics are value-comparison: both sides must be
         single values; multi-valued operand raises CXER0103; no type
         coercion. This is the deliberate divergence from XPath's
         general-comparison default (XPath's #1 gotcha — sequences
         silently matching any pair)."""]

    [body
      """**Paths on bindings.** A CXPath rooted at a binding navigates
         from that value:"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """$u/name           [# direct child element                       #]
         $u/*              [# all direct children                        #]
         $u//item          [# descendant                                 #]
         $u/ancestor::section   [# explicit axis                         #]
         $u@active         [# attribute access                           #]
         $u.region         [# map-key access                             #]"""]

    [body
      """**Paths compose with every construct.** This is the unified
         vocabulary principle — one selector, every direction:"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[?for $u :in //user[@active=true] :yield render($u)]
         [?if //admin :then [admin-panel] :else ()]
         [?match $n :case //prose :yield [p $n]]
         [?let $cfg = //meta/@config :in render($cfg)]
         [?modify $doc //user/@status :set 'verified']
         count(//user[@active=true])"""]

    [note
      """The full XPath 3.1 alignment table — what is adopted, what is
         deliberately not, what is on the XPath 4.0 watch list — is in
         [`spec/cxpath_alignment.md`](../../spec/cxpath_alignment.md)."""]]

  [child id=patterns n=5.4 title='Patterns and matching'
    [intro
      """A **pattern** is a literal CX subtree extended with binding
         holes (`$name`), wildcards (`*`, `_`), type guards
         (`:TypeName`), and scalar literals. Patterns appear in
         `[?for]`'s first generator, in `[?match]` arms, and in
         destructuring `[?let]`. The full pattern grammar is
         [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md) §5."""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[# element shape pattern                #]
         [user :email $e]                       [# binds $e to email attr #]
         [user @active=true $u]                 [# attr filter + bind     #]

         [# scalar literal pattern (ADR 0029)    #]
         :case 200 :yield :ok
         :case 'admin' :yield :elevated
         :case true :yield :enabled

         [# wildcards                            #]
         [user *]                               [# any user, any body     #]
         _                                      [# any value (binds none) #]

         [# type guard                           #]
         [config :ServerConfig $c]              [# schema-typed bind      #]"""]

    [body
      """**Binding semantics depend on context.** A pattern `[NAME $x]`
         **without** attribute predicates auto-unwraps the body — text
         body binds the string, scalar body binds the scalar, empty
         body binds the element itself. A pattern `[NAME @attr… $x]`
         **with** attribute predicates always binds `$x` to the
         matched element. The rationale is in
         [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md) §5.2 rule 5."""]]

  [child id=bindings n=5.5 title='Bindings and paths'
    [intro
      """A binding is a name introduced by `[?let]`, `[?for]`,
         `[?fn]`, or `[?match]` pattern. Bindings are referenced via
         `$name`. They are immutable — there is no rebinding (per CX's
         pure-functional discipline)."""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[?let $admins = //user[@role='admin']
            :in [?for $a :in $admins :yield $a@email]]"""]

    [body
      """A binding's value is whatever expression produced it.
         Navigation from a binding uses CXPath syntax (see [[cxpath]]
         under "Paths on bindings"). The path operators that work on
         bindings are the same operators that work on the implicit
         document — one vocabulary."""]]

  [child id=constructs n=5.6 title='Core constructs'
    [intro
      """The six core constructs are how every CX program is built.
         Each is documented in the following sub-sections."""]

    [child id=for n=5.6.1 title='[?for] — iteration'
      [body
        """`[?for]` is the iteration / comprehension primitive. The
           pattern in `:in` position is either a pattern (matches the
           generator), a CXPath (yields a sequence to iterate), or any
           expression that evaluates to a sequence."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[# CXPath generator                                           #]
           [?for $u :in //user :yield $u@email]

           [# Pattern generator (the `[?find]` replacement)              #]
           [?for [user @active=true $u] :yield $u@email]

           [# Filter with :where                                         #]
           [?for $u :in //user :where ($u@age >= 18) :yield $u]

           [# Parallel evaluation                                        #]
           [?for $r :in //request :par :yield process($r)]

           [# Streaming over large input                                 #]
           [?for $row :in //logs/row :stream :yield extract($row)]"""]
      [body
        """`:par` and `:stream` are evaluation modifiers — same surface,
           different scheduler. Full semantics in
           [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md) §7."""]]

    [child id=match n=5.6.2 title='[?match] — dispatch'
      [body
        """`[?match]` is pattern dispatch. v0.8.0 supports two forms:
           **2-arg destructure** (single arm, asserts the value
           matches) and **multi-arm** (first-match-wins across N
           arms). The multi-arm form is the major addition for v0.8.0
           — see ADR
           [0029](../../spec/decisions/0029-match-heterogeneous-arms.md)."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[# 2-arg form — asserts the value matches PAT or raises      #]
           [?match $result [ok $v] :yield $v]

           [# Multi-arm form — first-match-wins                          #]
           [?match $node
             :case [prose $p]   :yield [p $p]
             :case [code $c]    :yield [pre $c]
             :case //heading    :yield [h2 $node]
             :else :yield ()]

           [# SQL Searched-CASE — predicate-only mode                    #]
           [?match
             :when ($x > 100)   :yield :big
             :when ($y > 50)    :yield :medium
             :else :yield :small]

           [# Guards on :case arms                                       #]
           [?match $req
             :case [http-get $u] :where (allowed($u)) :yield [route $u]
             :else :yield :denied]"""]
      [body
        """Arm patterns include element shapes, scalar literals,
           CXPath paths, and the `_` wildcard. The no-match behavior
           differs by form: the 2-arg form raises `CXER0100`; the
           multi-arm form yields `()` if there is no `:else`."""]]

    [child id=if n=5.6.3 title='[?if] — boolean branch'
      [body
        """`[?if cond :then E1 :else E2]` is the explicit boolean
           branch. The condition is evaluated with the EBV
           (effective-boolean-value) rule from
           [`spec/cxdm.md`](../../spec/cxdm.md) §4.6: empty sequence
           is false, single-item sequence containing `false` / `0` /
           `null` / `''` is false, everything else is true."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?if //admin :then [admin-panel] :else [user-panel]]
           [?if ($age >= 18) :then :adult :else :minor]"""]
      [body
        """For multi-way conditionals prefer `[?match]` multi-arm.
           `[?if]` stays separate from `[?match]` because they have
           different contracts — `[?if]` uses EBV, `[?match] :case`
           does **not** (it tests strictly)."""]]

    [child id=let n=5.6.4 title='[?let] — binding'
      [body
        """`[?let]` introduces a local binding. The bound name is in
           scope inside `:in`."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?let $config = //meta/@config
             :in render($config)]

           [# Destructuring let — pattern in binding position           #]
           [?let [user @id=1 $admin] = //user[@id=1]
             :in $admin@email]"""]]

    [child id=modify n=5.6.5 title='[?modify] — pure-functional updates'
      [body
        """`[?modify]` is the v0.8.0 update directive. It takes a
           document (or sub-tree), a CXPath focus, and one or more
           action slots; it returns a **new** document with the action
           applied at every focus match. The original is unchanged
           (pure-functional). See ADR
           [0030](../../spec/decisions/0030-modify-pure-functional-updates.md)."""]

      [body
        """The **eleven action slots** are:"""]

      [table columns=(action, description)
        [row action=':set V' description='Replace matched value or attribute']
        [row action=':delete' description='Remove matched node or attribute']
        [row action=':using F' description='Apply function F to each match']
        [row action=':rename N' description='Rename element (keep attrs + body)']
        [row action=':set-attr N V' description='Add or overwrite attribute']
        [row action=':delete-attr N' description='Remove attribute by name']
        [row action=':append V' description='Add child at end of body']
        [row action=':prepend V' description='Add child at start of body']
        [row action=':insert-before V' description='New sibling before match']
        [row action=':insert-after V' description='New sibling after match']
        [row action=':replace V' description='Replace entire node with V']]

      [example lang=cx
        """[# Scalar attribute update                                    #]
           [?modify $doc //user[@id=1]/@name :set 'Alice']

           [# Delete matching nodes                                      #]
           [?modify $doc //user[@active=false] :delete]

           [# Transform with :using                                      #]
           [?modify $doc //price :using [?fn $p :body (* $p 1.1)]]

           [# Append a child                                             #]
           [?modify $doc //section[@id='intro'] :append [para 'Added.']]

           [# Multi-step pipeline                                        #]
           $doc
             | [?modify //user/@status :set 'verified']
             | [?modify //user[@banned=true] :delete]"""]

      [body
        """**Structural sharing.** `[?modify]` does not copy the whole
           tree. Only the spine from root to each matched node is
           copied; unchanged branches are shared with the original.
           For a 10 MB document with one matched node, the new heap
           cost is < 1 KB per matched node (gate 30.5)."""]

      [body
        """**Multi-match.** The focus path selects a sequence; the
           action applies to every node in that sequence. Zero matches
           returns the original unchanged (not an error)."""]]

    [child id=pipe n=5.6.6 title='Pipelines and composition'
      [body
        """The pipe operator `|` threads the output of one expression
           into the input of the next. Pipelines compose `[?modify]`,
           `[?for]`, builtins, and user-defined functions into
           readable top-to-bottom data flow."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """//user[@active=true]
           | filter-by-region('EU')
           | sort-by(@last-login)
           | take(10)"""]
      [body
        """`|` is sugar for `[?pipe]` — the canonical directive form
           is in the directive registry but the operator form is what
           you write."""]]]

  [child id=builtins n=5.7 title='Built-in functions'
    [intro
      """Built-ins are the standard library at the expression layer.
         Full table in [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md) §6.5; the
         subsections below cover the common ones."""]

    [child id=sequence-builtins n=5.7.1 title='Sequence built-ins'
      [body
        """`count(seq)`, `length(seq)`, `first(seq)`, `last(seq)`,
           `reverse(seq)`, `distinct(seq)`, `take(seq, n)`,
           `drop(seq, n)`, `sort-by(seq, key)`, `group-by(seq, key)`."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """count(//user[@active=true])
           first(//section)
           sort-by(//user, @last-login)"""]]

    [child id=string-builtins n=5.7.2 title='String built-ins'
      [body
        """`upcase(s)`, `lowcase(s)`, `trim(s)`, `split(s, sep)`,
           `concat(a, b, ...)` (or `a || b`),
           `starts-with(s, prefix)`, `ends-with(s, suffix)`,
           `contains(s, substr)`, `replace(s, find, repl)`,
           `format(template, args...)`."""]]

    [child id=numeric-builtins n=5.7.3 title='Numeric built-ins'
      [body
        """`sum(seq)`, `min(seq)`, `max(seq)`, `avg(seq)`,
           `abs(n)`, `floor(n)`, `ceil(n)`, `round(n)`, `pow(b, e)`,
           `to-int(v)`, `to-float(v)`, `to-string(v)`."""]]

    [child id=logical-builtins n=5.7.4 title='Logical built-ins'
      [body
        """`and(a, b, ...)`, `or(a, b, ...)`, `not(v)`,
           `some(seq, fn)`, `every(seq, fn)`,
           `if(c, t, e)` (expression form of `[?if]`),
           `instance-of(v, type)`."""]]

    [child id=accessor-builtins n=5.7.5 title='Node-accessor built-ins'
      [body
        """`name($n)`, `local-name($n)`, `kind($n)`, `attrs($n)`,
           `children($n)`, `body($n)`, `parent($n)`, `root($n)`.
           These are the script-level equivalents of the Layer-1
           binding methods (see [[layer-1]])."""]]]

  [child id=errors n=5.8 title='Errors as values'
    [intro
      """CX treats errors as ordinary values. An error is a CX element
         with kind `cx-err:CXERnnnn`. It can be passed around, pattern
         matched, transformed, and recovered from. Full error registry
         in [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md) §9.5."""]

    [body
      """The propagation operators are postfix `?` and `!`. Reading:
         `f($x)?` — call `f`; if it errored, propagate the error
         unchanged through the surrounding directive. `f($x)!` — call
         `f`; if it errored, raise immediately as a host exception."""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[?try
           [?let $u = lookup-user($id)?
             :in render($u)]
           :on-error :CXER0204 :yield [error 'no such user']
           :on-error _ :yield [error 'lookup failed']]"""]

    [body
      """The `[?try]` block names recoverable error codes via
         `:on-error CODE` arms; the `_` wildcard catches anything
         else. The full error-code namespace is reserved per category
         — for code-language errors specifically, CXER0100 through
         CXER0299. See [[concepts]] for the namespace layout."""]]

  [child id=concurrency n=5.9 title='Concurrency'
    [intro
      """CX has three concurrency surfaces, all spelled as directives.
         They build on the pure-functional core: every channel send,
         every worker call, every async await sees only immutable
         values, so there is no shared-mutable-state hazard. Spec:
         [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md) §10.4 + §10.5."""]

    [child id=services n=5.9.1 title='Services and clients'
      [body
        """`[?service]` declares a named HTTP service with resource
           handlers. `[?http-client]` is its client side. Resources
           handle requests and return responses; the bodies are
           ordinary CX code."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?service users
           [resource :get '/users/:id'
             [?let $u = //user[@id=$req.params.id]
               :in [response :status 200 :body $u]]]]"""]
      [body
        """The service surface (request/response shapes, lifecycle
           directives `[?stop]` / `[?wait-for]`, error codes) is
           specified in [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md) §10.3."""]]

    [child id=workers n=5.9.2 title='Workers and channels'
      [body
        """`[?worker]` spawns a worker task; `[?channel]` creates a
           bounded message queue; `[?send]` and `[?receive]` move
           values across. `[?select]` is the multi-way receive."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?let $ch = [?channel :capacity 100]
           :in [?for $i :in (1 to 10) :par :yield
                  [?send $ch process($i)]]]"""]]

    [child id=async n=5.9.3 title='Async and await'
      [body
        """`[?async]` returns a future; `[?await]` blocks for it;
           `[?await-all]` joins many; `[?await-any]` takes the first
           to complete; `[?await-race]` is `await-any` with the rest
           cancelled. `[?cancel]` triggers cooperative cancellation;
           `[?check-cancel]` polls."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?let $f1 = [?async fetch(url1)]
                $f2 = [?async fetch(url2)]
           :in [?await-all $f1 $f2]]"""]]]

  [child id=resilience n=5.10 title='Resilience directives'
    [intro
      """The six resilience directives wrap an inner expression with a
         failure-handling policy. They are not new control-flow
         primitives — they are normal CX code with cleanly-named
         policies. Spec: [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md) §10.2."""]

    [list
      [item """`[?retry]` — re-run on failure with backoff."""]
      [item """`[?timeout]` — bound execution time."""]
      [item """`[?circuit-breaker]` — open on N consecutive failures."""]
      [item """`[?fallback]` — return a default on failure."""]
      [item """`[?rate-limit]` — bound calls per interval."""]
      [item """`[?bulkhead]` — bound concurrent calls."""]]

    [example lang=cx
      """[?retry :max 3 :backoff exponential
         [?timeout :ms 5000
           [?http-get 'https://api.example.com/users']]]"""]

    [body
      """Resilience directives compose: stack them around any
         expression in any order. The semantics under composition are
         documented in [`spec/code.md`](../../spec/code.md) §10.2.8."""]]]
